# `self.items[self.pick()]` reads `self.items` before `pick` runs. `pick`
# writes nothing the caller can reach, so the read moves after the call
# and reads one element in place, with no copy of the list, as
# `i := self.pick()` then `self.items[i]` does (#1153). `bump` writes
# `self`, and `drain` writes the same stack through a `*mut Stack`, so a
# read across either keeps its copy of the list. A dev build has no
# summary of what a callee writes, so every call there keeps the copy.
